The agent means to stop without buying, believing nothing fits. Check the records shown below against the requirements, using these reading rules: For a requirement R on property P with value V: a field of the listing settles P whenever its key K names P (K spelled any way; one list member suffices; synonyms are fine and units may be converted; a value that includes V's words is fine, as a shade word followed by the wanted colour satisfies that colour; a limit includes its own boundary); a value filed under a key prefixing P with the item's own kind (on a backpack, a key for the backpack's colour) still counts though the bare key for P disagrees; a key for another property, however related, decides nothing (a strap's colour says nothing about the main body's colour); with no field naming P, V stated under some other field, or in the title or description, is enough, but a lone figure that never says what it measures is worth nothing; when R is a bound expressed with a unit (a measured limit), the only attribute that can settle it is one whose name has some word in common with P, and a figure under an attribute for some other dimension, or solely in the title, leaves R unknown, so the listing fails R; an attribute whose name is P in plural form and whose value is a list enumerates the variants the seller offers, and such a list does not give this particular listing's value whenever the title names some other member of it; if the category label states R, that category meets R; the category label has to match the item; a requirement to differ from another listing's value is taken as met, except where the records reveal that very value; a listing that is out of stock, or that goes over the budget, is not a fit; the binding requirement list overrides the request text, and any requirement flagged DROPPED is void.
ROW records come only from a search row: nobody has read their page (the fields), hence on any property the row itself does not show, such a record may be judged neither a pass nor a fail. Reply with one JSON object only: "fits" (an object whose keys are item numbers and whose values list the ids that seem to satisfy all requirements, lowest price first, 4 at most), "open" (ids of ROW listings whose kind was requested and that are within budget, whose page might prove a fit, likeliest first, 6 at most) and "reason" (one sentence).

REQUEST:
Alright, I'm wanting a hoodie or sweatshirt, and the style should be youth fashion (I'll check it myself, don't you worry). And I only want something y'all actually have on hand, not just listed on a website somewhere like a trail that's on the map but washed out. Nothing over 660 cts, y'all (student loans ain't gonna pay themselves), because every penny counts for me and that's the top of my climb. Total, you can send me 3 messages and no more than that. My sister asked me to grab her one, and she reads every tag like a trail map (bless her). Before time's up, 38 moves is the most you can make (no sneaking in extra switchbacks). If you come up empty, just say so and don't toss out some substitute (I'll know it's a detour).

REQUIREMENTS NOW LIVE:
- item: hoodie or sweatshirt, quantity 1; requirements: style: youth fashion; material: nylon
- budget: at most 660 cents in total
- pick: any listing meeting every requirement
- in stock only
- if nothing fits, the episode ends without naming or proposing any listing
- listing attributes: datasheet_for_item shows them; view_listing show none; view_listing display a listing's page, the page meant whenever a chain is followed or a page is checked

